findings.body:
The quicklist branch changed from `elesize/elecount*ql->count` to `elesize/samples*ql->count`. `elesize` accumulates per-node sizes, and `elecount` accumulates the element counts of those nodes. `ql->count` is the total number of elements in the list. The original expression gives an average size per element multiplied by the element total, which is correct. The new expression gives an average size per node multiplied by the element total, so it overestimates by roughly the number of elements per node. For a typical multi-node list, MEMORY USAGE can report several times the real size. This change is not needed for the kvobj key fix, and the new test does not exercise lists, so nothing catches it.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1230-1240"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is an unrelated arithmetic change hidden in a refactor. Revert it to the `elecount` divisor.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1239-1239"}
            asize += (double)elesize/elecount*ql->count;

The stream branch changed `lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1)` to `lpsize * s->rax->numele`. The code after the multiply adds the real size of the last listpack separately, via `zmalloc_size(ri.data)` after `raxSeek(&ri,"$")`. The original `numele-1` was there so that the average covers N-1 listpacks and the last one is counted once, exactly. With `numele`, the last listpack is counted once as an average and again as its real size. Every stream with more listpacks than `sample_size` now gets one extra average listpack, and streams with many macro nodes are overcounted proportionally. This is also not part of the key-accounting fix.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1326-1336"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Restore the original `numele-1`. The comment above the block already describes that N-1 plus the real last node model.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1330-1330"}
            asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1);

The new base is `malloc_usable_size((void *)o)`, but the rest of object.c, and the PR description, use `zmalloc_size()`. The difference matters. `zmalloc_size()` is the allocator-aware accessor, and it adjusts for the `PREFIX_SIZE` header that zmalloc adds on platforms without `HAVE_MALLOC_SIZE`. `src/zmalloc.c` shows `zmalloc_size()` reading `PREFIX_SIZE` bytes before the pointer for that case. Passing the pointer returned by zmalloc straight to `malloc_usable_size` is only valid when the zmalloc pointer is the raw malloc pointer, that is, when `PREFIX_SIZE` is 0. On other builds, the call reads from the wrong address (undefined behavior, and possibly a crash or a wrong size). Using `zmalloc_size` keeps this consistent with the other branches and with the allocator layer.

